        <body>
            <div xml:id="d206">

                <pb facs="0404" n="1364" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1364"/>

                <head>No. 919.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Pratt to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Teheran</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-09-28"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 28,
                        1888</date>. (Received November 5.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 291.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In my No. 32, consular series of the 4th
                    instant, I had the honor to inform the Department on the subject of two Persian
                    railways; one, recently completed between Teheran and Shat-Abdoul-Azim, and the
                    other in course of construction from the town of Amol to Mahmudabad, on the
                    Caspian Sea.</p>
                <p>I have now the honor to report that information has reached me through most
                    trustworthy private sources that the Shah’s Government has just been asked by
                    the syndicate which constructed the Shat-Abdoul-Azim <pb facs="0405" n="1365"
                        xml:id="pg1365"/> line to cede it, in addition, the exclusive right to
                    connect by rail the cities of Koum, Teheran, and Casrine, with the intention of
                    seeking later on the further grant to extend this system from the latter point
                    northward to the Caspian Sea and from the former southward to the Persian
                    Gulf.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. Spencer Pratt.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
